"274 b. Illicit contract between claim- ant and recipient. When a claimant seeks restitution for benefits conferred under a contract that is illegal or un- enforceable for reasons of public policy, equitable disqualification by the rule of this section may be a decisive ele- ment in the resolution of the claim. See § 32(3). . . . If the claimant has rendered a valu- able performance to the recipient in a prohibited transaction, the court must decide—if the question is not governed by the language and objectives of the relevant statute—whether the appro- priate sanctions for violation include forfeiture of what would otherwise be the claimant’s entitlement to restitu- tion. This question is frequently ad- dressed by asking whether the claim- ant is equitably disqualified to assert the restitution claim. . . . The relative culpability of the claimant and the recipient in the transaction between them will sometimes, but not always, be taken into account. . . . The consequences for restitution are the same when the claimant has conferred a benefit under an agree- ment with the recipient that (while not inherently wrongful) was entered into for a wrongful purpose. . . . c. Restitution from an innocent recipient. . . . The potential reach of equitable disqualification is . . . broad- er than a mere refusal to enforce or relieve against illegal transactions. Rather, the claimant’s misconduct may lead a court to deny relief to which a claimant would ordinarily be entitled, based on any of the headings of liability in restitution. . . . § 63 RESTATEMENT OF RESTITUTION AND UNJUST ENRICHMENT"